From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 09:17:57 -0400
Subject: paint the window Stedding's colours: sand light, blue-to-plum dark
 gradient

From the operator: light mode #C8B377, dark mode a top-left to bottom-right
gradient from #21263A to #31243A.

Two layers. A colour mixer, added after every Chromium mixer and before custom
themes, sets the flat palette (frame, toolbar) so everything that derives a
colour derives it from ours. The gradient itself cannot come from a mixer --
mixers hand out single colours -- so BrowserView paints a
SteddingWindowBackground: the gradient in dark, flat sand in light.

For the gradient to read as one surface, the sidebar, the top container and the
content mat paint nothing in Arc mode and let it show through. Nothing is
removed, only made invisible: the first attempt set the sidebar's background to
null and crashed at startup, because the layout dereferences that
CustomCornersBackground unconditionally for corner cutouts. Invisible objects
keep every such caller safe.

Verified by pixel probe: dark reads (33,38,57) at the top-left drifting redder
toward the bottom, continuous through sidebar, toolbar and mat; light reads
sand within colour-profile rounding. All 22 tests pass.

Why: the operator picked the colours; one ground with no seams is what makes
the chrome feel like one object.
Removable when: Stedding grows real theme settings, where these become the
defaults rather than the only choice.
